ed as read-only first, without a code, and so is the owner of a read-only account.  `code_quality.enabled: true` turns on LavaFlow Code Quality, which reviews the lines each production deploy of a frontend changes and turns what it confirms into issues and pull requests. Function deploys ask for no scan yet. It needs the project's `lavaflow.code_quality` capability: while Code Quality isn't released here the save answers `400` with code `feature_unavailable`, and while the owner's plan allows no Code Quality findings it answers `403` with code `plan_required`. Only the owner can turn it on, with the check below. Turning it off always works, and drops the deploys waiting to be scanned.  `autonomy: merge` needs the project's `lavaflow.merge` capability. Without it, a save that sets `autonomy` to `merge`, or changes `merge_policy`, answers `400` with code `feature_unavailable`. A save that sends the saved values again passes this check, though it can still need the owner's check below, and a project at `merge` can still change to another autonomy. The save doesn't read the repository's branch rules: LavaFlow merges only into a base branch that requires at least one status check, and holds every fix with `no_required_checks` on any other.  Changing `autonomy` to `merge` lets LavaFlow merge with no one else looking. So does turning `enabled` on while `autonomy` stays `merge`, and changing `merge_policy`, to any value, while `autonomy` is, or becomes, `merge`. Turning `allow_ungated_workflows` on lets LavaFlow open a fix where a workflow could run it with the repository's secrets before a person approves it. Turning `code_quality` on starts work on every frontend production deploy with no incident behind it. Only the owner can make these changes, with a credential of their own that expires: a `volcano login` token whose sign-in is live, a live Volcano dashboard sign-in, or a platform token with an expiry. The owner's GitHub account must also be able to push to the project's repository. A project access token, MCP, a platform token that never expires, a sign-in that ended, an owner who can't push, and a project with no repository get `403` with code `person_required`, and nothing is saved. Turning LavaFlow, `allow_ungated_workflows` or `code_quality` off needs no check. Nothing is saved either when the push check can't be answered (`429` or `503`, with `Retry-After`). When the project points at another repository, reaches its repository through another Volcano GitHub App installation, or points at none, `autonomy` changes from `merge` to `open_pr`; pointing back doesn't change it back. Reconnecting after a reinstall of the App counts as another installation.  Turning LavaFlow on needs a plan that includes it (`403`, code `plan_required`). LavaFlow is available where the project's `lavaflow` capability is, which includes the owner's plan. Where it is not, a project with LavaFlow records can still turn LavaFlow, Code Quality or both off: a save that sends `enabled: false`, `code_quality: {enabled: false}` or both, and otherwise only `allow_ungated_workflows: false` sent with `enabled`. Any other save, and any save for a project without records, answers `404` with code `feature_unavailable` while LavaFlow is not offered to the project, and `403` with code `plan_required` when it is but the owner's plan does not include it. 
